              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+group"} free group
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              is itself a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+group"} free group
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              is free on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              generators and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
              has index
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
              is free on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mi k
                    %mo −
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n k - n + 1
              generators.
          %p
            The second part of this yields what is called the
            = succeed "." do
              %em Schreier index formula
          %p This has a number of different proofs. The first proof, perhaps nowadays the most familiar proof, is based on covering space theory (in particular, covering spaces of topological graphs). The second proof is quite similar in spirit but is based on groupoids (another way of viewing homotopy 1-types), and has the advantage that it circumvents the point-set topology considerations inherent to covering space theory.
          .proof
            %h6#topological_proof_sketch Topological Proof (sketch)
            %p
              %strong
                of theorem
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#NielsenSchreierTheorem"}
            %p
              A free group
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mo =
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G = F(S)
              is, by the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/van+Kampen+theorem"} van Kampen theorem
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+group"} fundamental group
              of a bouquet
              %sup#fnref:1
                %a{:href => "#fn:1", :rel => "footnote"} 1
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              many circles, which is in particular a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+space"} connected
              1-dimensional
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CW-complex"} CW-complex
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              (in simpler language, a connected graph). By general
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covering+space"} covering space
              theory, given a (pointed) connected space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              with
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1(X, x) = G
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H \subseteq G
              are in bijective correspondence with isomorphism classes of connected covering spaces
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo :
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi E
                      %mo ,
                      %mi e
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p: (E, e) \to (X, x)
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi E
                      %mo ,
                      %mi e
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≅
                      %mi H
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1(E, e) \cong H
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
              of a connected graph
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is also a connected graph. But any connected graph is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+equivalence"} homotopy equivalent
              to a bouquet of circles, whose fundamental group is a free group. Thus
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi E
                    %mo ,
                    %mi e
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1(E, e)
              is a free group.
            %p
              The second statement is proved by observing that the Euler characteristic of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi χ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                      %mo −
                      %mi ρ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi G
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi(X) = 1 - \rho(G)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ρ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho(G)
              is the number of generators of the free group
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi χ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi n
                    %mi χ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi(E) = n\chi(X)
              if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi E
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \colon E \to X
              is a covering space with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              points in each fiber.
            %p
              Full details may be found in
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "#Maybook"} May
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
              contains a maximal
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tree"} tree
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              (using
              = succeed ")," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Zorn%27s+lemma"} Zorn's lemma
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                    %mo →
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E \to E/T
              is a homotopy equivalence, and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E/T
              is a bouquet of circles.
          %p
            This topological proof can be reformulated in more algebraic language, using a little groupoid theory (groupoids being
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+n-type"} homotopy 1-types
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+groupoid"} action groupoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⫽
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \sslash G
            formed from a
            = succeed "-set" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          .proof
            %h6#groupoidal_proof Groupoidal Proof
            %p
              %strong
                of theorem
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#NielsenSchreierTheorem"}
            %p
              By the discussion at
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/free+groupoid#FundamentalGroup"} free groupoid – fundamental group
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+group"} free group
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(S)
              as the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+group"} fundamental group
              of a homotopy 1-type which is freely built from a single vertex and one loop from that vertex to itself for each element in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+groupoid"} free groupoid
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                    %mo ⫽
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *\sslash F(S)
              on this
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+graph"} directed graph
              (a bouquet of circles). It is a classical fact (see at
              = succeed ")" do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+principal+bundle"} universal principal bundle
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+cover"} universal cover
              of this is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contractible"} contractible
              groupoid
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi E
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{E}(F(S))
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+groupoid"} action groupoid
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⫽
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(S)\sslash F(S)
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(S)
              acting on itself from the right and that its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient"} quotient
              by the
              = succeed "-action" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(S)
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(S)
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi H
                      %mo ↪
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H \hookrightarrow F(S)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
              (meaning simply that at any point or object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              of the groupoid, the group
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hom(x, x)
              is isomorphic to
              = succeed ")." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi H
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            %p
              Thus, consider the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient"} quotient
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi H
                      %mo \
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi E
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≃
                      %mi H
                      %mo \
                      %mo \
                      %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H \backslash \mathbf{E}F(S) \simeq H \backslash \backslash *
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+the"} the
              connected groupoid whose
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+group"} fundamental group
              is
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 1
                      %mo =
                      %mi H
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1 = H
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+groupoids"} free groupoids
              discussed at
              %em
                %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/free+groupoid#FundamentalGroup"} free groupoid – fundamental group
              it is sufficient to show that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                    %mo \
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi E
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H \backslash \mathbf{E}F(S)
              is isomorphic to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+groupoid"} free groupoid
              on a connected
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+graph"} directed graph
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi E
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{E}F(S)
              itself is the free groupoid on a directed graph, namely on the
              %em action graph
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⫽
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(S)\sslash S
              (which is the same as the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cayley+graph"} Cayley graph
              given by a set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              of generators and no relations), and we may consider the quotient graph
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi H
                      %mo \
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⫽
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H \backslash (F(S) \sslash S)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              preserves this quotient. Thus we have
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                    %mo \
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi E
                    %mi F
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %mi S
                      %mo )
                    %mo =
                    %mi H
                    %mo \
                    %mi F
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %mi F
                      %mrow
                        %mo (
                        %mi S
                        %mo )
                      %mo ⫽
                      %mi S
                      %mo )
                    %mo =
                    %mi F
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %mrow
                        %mo (
                        %mi H
                        %mo \
                        %mi F
                        %mrow
                          %mo (
                          %mi S
                          %mo )
                        %mo )
                      %mo ⫽
                      %mi S
                      %mo )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    H \backslash \mathbf{E}F\left(S\right) = H  \backslash F\left( F\left(S\right)\sslash S \right) =  F \left(\left(H \backslash F\left(S\right)\right)\sslash S\right)
                    \\,.
            %p as desired.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              The original algebraic proof of theorem
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#NielsenSchreierTheorem"}
              was rather long and complicated, based on Nielsen’s method of short cancellations in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/combinatorial+group+theory"} combinatorial group theory
              based on words (Nielsen’s transformations of words, word length functions, …). The class of
              %em ‘projective groups’
              ( that is, in this context,
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+object"} projective object
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grp"} the category of groups
            %p
              The above simple
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy-theoretic
              proof was indicated in (
              %a{:href => "#Higgins"}> Higgins
              ), also (
              %a{:href => "#GilbrtPorter"}> Gilbert-Porter
              ).
          %h2#related_entries Related entries
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cayley+graph"} Cayley graph
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "free+module#SubmodulesOfFreeModules"} submodules of a free module
          #DedekindTheorem.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_2 Theorem
            %p
              %strong (Dedekind’s theorem)
            %p
              Every subgroup of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+abelian+group"} free abelian group
              is itself a free abelian group.
          %p
            See at
            %em
              %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/principal+ideal+domain#StructureTheoryOfModules"} pid - Structure theory of modules
            for details.
          %h2#references References
          %p
            The restricted statement that every subgroup of a free
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+group"} abelian group
            is itself free was originally given by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Richard+Dedekind"} Richard Dedekind
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jakob+Nielsen"} Jakob Nielsen
            proved the statement for finitely-generated subgroups in 1921. The full theorem was proven in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Otto+Schreier"}> Otto Schreier
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Die Untergruppen der freien Gruppen
              %strong 5
              (1927) 161–183 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02952517"}> doi:10.1007/BF02952517
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p The topological proof is due to
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Reinhold+Baer"}> Reinhold Baer
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Friedrich+Levi"} Friedrich Levi
              = succeed "," do
                %em Freie Produkte und ihre Untergruppen
          %p
            and another one due to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean-Pierre+Serre"} Jean-Pierre Serre
          %p Versions of the topological proof are given in many places. One is
          %ul#Maybook
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+May"}> Peter May
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em A Concise Course in Algebraic Topology
              = succeed ")," do
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.uchicago.edu/~may/CONCISE/ConciseRevised.pdf"} pdf
          %p Another can be found reviewed towards the end of
          %ul
            %li#GilbertPorter
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/N.+D.+Gilbert"}> N. D. Gilbert
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/T.+Porter"} T. Porter
              = succeed "," do
                %em Knots and Surfaces
          %p Textbook account:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mark+A.+Armstrong"}> Mark A. Armstrong
              , chapter 28 of:
              = succeed "," do
                %em Groups and Symmetry
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4757-4034-9"} doi:10.1007/978-1-4757-4034-9
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://superoles.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/10/lluvia.pdf"} pdf
          %p Further related texts::
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                H. Zieschang,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Über die Nielsensche Kürzungsmethode in freien Produkten mit Amalgam
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 10
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wilhelm+Magnus"}> Wilhelm Magnus
                , A. Karras, D. Solitar,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Combinatorial group theory: Presentation of groups in terms of generators and relations
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.maa.org/press/maa-reviews/combinatorial-group-theory-presentations-of-groups-in-terms-of-generators-and-relations"} ISBN:0-486-43830-9
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://books.google.com/books?hl=en&lr=&id=1LW4s1RDRHQC&oi=fnd&pg=PP1&ots=WszNa-0hEz&sig=Hn_MTbyLcEreBBNZ8jJtQUVKlZk"} GoogleBooks
            %li
              %p
                R. Lyndon, P. Schupp,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Combinatorial group theory
          %p
            A
            = succeed "-based" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
          %ul
            %li#Higgins
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Philip+Higgins"}> P. J. Higgins
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Categories and groupoids
              %a{:href => "http://www.emis.de/journals/TAC/reprints/articles/7/tr7abs.html"} Repr. Theory Appl. Categ., 1 – 178
              , ISSN:1201-561X, reprint of the 1971 original
              = succeed "," do
                %em Notes on categories and groupoids
              %p Reinhold, London; MR0327946) with a new preface by the author.
          %p Similar material can also be found in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/R.+Brown"}> R. Brown
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Topology and groupoids
          %p
            Discussion in
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
